---
title: ChromaClack - The Good The Bad and The Ugly
publishedDate: 2026-09-24
excerpt: >-
  A retrospective on two weeks of failed effort in trying to implement a multi
  axis input element, that would allow a user to explore colour theory.
---
> "Never promise to do the possible. Anyone could do the possible. You should promise to do the impossible, because sometimes the impossible was possible, if you could find the right way, and at least you could often extend the limits of the possible. And if you failed, well, it had been impossible."  — Terry Pratchett, *Going Postal*

After getting this blog site up and running, and my fancy pants Hello World project finished, I decided to pick something more difficult for my next project. I wanted to test the limits of what I could achieve when coding with AI, so I decided to pick a domain that I felt would be challenging and outside of the comfort zone. The idea I came up with was ChromaClack, a toy app that allows the user to explore colour theory through a single UI element.

This UI element is comprised of three modes of interaction, swiping up and down, left and right, hold and rotate. The idea was that the UI element would mimic a mechanical roledex in the first two, and a dimmer switch in the last. I felt this would be a particular challenge, because it would involve some decently complex math and raw css to handle the animation, and the spacial reasoning required to implement this would be pushing the boundaries of what an LLM can do. As you can seen in the video below, the idea is each axis of movement could be tied to Hue/Saturation/Light, or Red/Blue/Green, to let a user mix these values and see how the colour changes.

{% video
   src="/videos/posts/chroma-clack-the-good-the-bad-and-the-ugly/Screencast From 2026-09-24 09-06-10.mp4"
   autoPlay=true
   width="30%" /%}

I wanted to try out a tech stack that would give me a single source of truth for native Android, iOS, desktop and cloud based applications. After some discussion with Gemini I settled on this; A SvelteKit app, either sitting on of Tauri V2 or hosted on Vercel, with a common rust library, so any complicated backend logic can be shared between Vercel serverless rust functions, or executed by the Tauri V2 scaffolding. All of this in a monorepo using turborepo. It took a couple of days of wiring but eventually with copilots help we got a fully functioning system that was a single repo, that I could run on my Nothing phone directly, on Ubuntu and hosted on Vercel, all executing the exact same Svelte code on the front end, and the same common rust library on the back end. I'll admit it's a very convoluted solution, but I'll go into more detail in a future post on why I thought it was worth doing. This tech stack also added another layer of complexity which is the principal design goal for the project.
